      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "@{59b15c3e62dfd809eb1144d3|full_name}: My experience is leaving the checkbox on causes constant hitching even if time index is never being set.  This is true even when I don't experience desync normally while it's turned off.  Is there a best practice around using this option via SDK.  Will turning it on and off again at runtime have an effect, how long would it need to be left on, etc.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2021-07-11T01:57:28.355Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "(My proposed solutions are at the end)\nWith the new change for attempting to keep the audio and video in sync, I'm noticing a rather significant amount of hitching over time.\nIf I build and test with 2 clients and try to play some media (let's say it's a music video), just having them play without doing any system intensive tasks, they will become significantly out of network-sync due to audible hitching from the audio/video sync corrections.\nThis particular behavior was present on a previous version of my video player when it was trying to enforce a sync update just about every second.\nI also noticed [pic related] happening in the logs which further cemented my assumptions about what's going on.\nWhile this enforced AV sync keeps the video itself in-line, it breaks some syncing across clients for video players that have such features.\nPlease revisit the particular implementation of this AV sync enforcement.\nMy suggestions would be any of:\n1) Make the AV resync threshold configurable through the BaseVRCVideoPlayer interface.\n2) Expose a method to udon that does a one-off AV resync.\n3) Allow creators and/or udon to enable/disable the forced AV resync.\n4) All of the above",
